  "image_name": "1827ed17f6bbe54fe960f78c9bf5a679.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how a photoelectrochemical signal can arise at a biological membrane and contrasts two ways it is coupled to an electrode. The large schematic at the top treats the membrane as an electrical circuit with membrane voltage and transmembrane current, alongside interfacial elements such as capacitance and Faradaic impedance at the substrate. Light-sensitive components on the membrane generate changes in membrane potential and current, which are then transferred through the membrane–electrode interface. The diagram emphasizes that the measured substrate voltage depends not only on the membrane response itself but also on interfacial charging and charge-transfer pathways.\n\nThe lower cartoons summarize the two limiting mechanisms. In the capacitive case, illumination changes the membrane potential and redistributes charge near the electrode without actual redox chemistry across the interface; electrons in the working electrode shift to balance the changing electric field. In the Faradaic case, the light-induced membrane response is coupled to oxidation and reduction reactions in the electrolyte, producing real charge transfer between solution species and the electrode. Overall, the figure highlights that the same optical membrane excitation can be detected electrically either through non-Faradaic capacitive coupling or through Faradaic electrochemical currents.",
